WEBVTT

1
00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:20.943
Marius entered Cosette's garden each night by pushing aside one of the bars in the gate.

2
00:00:21.964 --> 00:00:25.126
For more than a month, they met among the trees and shrubbery.

3
00:00:25.726 --> 00:00:29.784
They told each other about their families and their pasts.

4
00:00:29.804 --> 00:00:35.889
Marius had fallen so madly in love, he had temporarily forgotten all about that strange night in the

5
00:00:35.929 --> 00:00:37.090
Thenardiers' apartment.

6
00:00:38.071 --> 00:00:42.495
He did not know at night what he had eaten for breakfast or what he had done all

7
00:00:42.535 --> 00:00:47.219
day. He lived only for the hours when he was able to see Cosette.

8
00:00:47.839 --> 00:00:49.060
Then he was in heaven.

9
00:00:50.221 --> 00:00:51.923
Jean Valjean suspected nothing.

10
00:00:52.483 --> 00:00:56.006
Cosette, though less distracted than Marius, was cheerful.

11
00:00:57.047 --> 00:01:00.405
She was happy, and that was enough to make Valjean happy.

12
00:01:01.345 --> 00:01:06.128
Marius always arrived after Valjean was in bed, so the two men never met.

13
00:01:06.828 --> 00:01:08.609
Marius never entered the house.

14
00:01:09.249 --> 00:01:11.930
He sat with Cosette in the garden and held her hand.

15
00:01:12.890 --> 00:01:18.533
One night in early June 1832, Marius arrived and found that Cosette was sad.

16
00:01:19.193 --> 00:01:21.574
She had been weeping and her eyes were red.

17
00:01:21.594 --> 00:01:23.555
"What's the matter?"

18
00:01:24.035 --> 00:01:25.256
asked Marius in alarm.

19
00:01:26.288 --> 00:01:29.131
She sat on a bench and he took his place beside her.

20
00:01:29.151 --> 00:01:34.835
"My father told me this morning that I must prepare to go away."

21
00:01:34.855 --> 00:01:36.517
"What do you mean?"

22
00:01:36.537 --> 00:01:42.561
"He said I should tidy up my affairs and be ready to leave Paris in a week.

23
00:01:43.662 --> 00:01:46.324
And oh, Marius, this is the worst part.

24
00:01:47.445 --> 00:01:50.268
Here Cosette had to stop so she wouldn't begin to cry.

25
00:01:50.288 --> 00:01:53.330
"He said that perhaps we would move to England!"

26
00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:55.292
"That's horrible!

27
00:01:55.944 --> 00:01:56.825
When would you come back?"

28
00:01:57.325 --> 00:01:58.166
"He didn't say."

29
00:01:58.186 --> 00:02:01.589
"So will you go to England?"

30
00:02:02.149 --> 00:02:02.950
asked Marius.

31
00:02:03.711 --> 00:02:06.433
Cosette's eyes were full of pain and confusion.

32
00:02:06.453 --> 00:02:08.915
"Why do you ask me that?

33
00:02:09.635 --> 00:02:13.859
If my father leaves for England, of course I must go with him."

34
00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:16.601
Marius shuddered.

35
00:02:16.621 --> 00:02:18.282
"This is all so sudden.

36
00:02:19.003 --> 00:02:20.784
Is there anything we can do to stop him?"

37
00:02:21.885 --> 00:02:24.788
Cosette shook her head, but then she began to smile.

38
00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:26.861
"There is one thing you could do.

39
00:02:27.881 --> 00:02:29.001
Follow me to England!"

40
00:02:29.021 --> 00:02:30.642
"Go with you?

41
00:02:31.122 --> 00:02:31.962
Are you insane?"

42
00:02:32.322 --> 00:02:33.302
Marius leaped up.

43
00:02:33.883 --> 00:02:36.323
"I don't have money for a passport, never mind a ticket.

44
00:02:37.444 --> 00:02:40.945
You only see me at night, so you don't realize how shabby my clothes are.

45
00:02:41.565 --> 00:02:43.845
If you saw me by day, you'd think I was a beggar."

46
00:02:44.826 --> 00:02:47.486
Marius leaned against a tree with his eyes closed.

47
00:02:48.007 --> 00:02:52.608
He stood a long time like that until he heard a small, stifled sound.

48
00:02:54.056 --> 00:02:55.457
It was Cosette sobbing.

49
00:02:56.057 --> 00:02:57.898
He fell on his knees in front of her.

50
00:02:57.918 --> 00:03:00.219
"Don't weep," he said.

51
00:03:00.239 --> 00:03:01.559
"Do you love me?"

52
00:03:01.579 --> 00:03:03.340
"You know that I do.

53
00:03:04.881 --> 00:03:05.761
Do you love me?"

54
00:03:05.781 --> 00:03:12.564
"Cosette, I've never given my word of honor to anyone because that's such a serious thing to do.

55
00:03:13.224 --> 00:03:16.305
But I swear that if you go away, I shall die!"

56
00:03:17.786 --> 00:03:20.107
Cosette was so shocked that she stopped crying.

57
00:03:20.127 --> 00:03:22.528
"Now listen," said Marius.

58
00:03:23.136 --> 00:03:24.457
"Don't expect me tomorrow night.

59
00:03:24.897 --> 00:03:27.999
I have something I must do, but I will return here the next night.

60
00:03:28.899 --> 00:03:32.621
In the meantime you should know my address in case you need to get in touch with me.

61
00:03:33.302 --> 00:03:37.384
I live with my friend, Courfeyrac, at 16 Rue de la Verrerie."

62
00:03:38.545 --> 00:03:41.827
He took out his penknife and scratched the address on the garden wall.

63
00:03:42.767 --> 00:03:46.689
"I will wait for you," said Cosette, taking his hands in hers.

64
00:03:47.750 --> 00:03:51.072
The next evening Marius set out for his grandfather's house.

65
00:03:51.752 --> 00:03:55.815
For four years Monsieur Gillenormand had hoped that Marius would return.

66
00:03:56.876 --> 00:04:01.679
But the old man was still stunned when a servant announced that Marius wished to see him.

67
00:04:01.699 --> 00:04:05.002
"Show him in," said Monsieur Gillenormand.

68
00:04:05.462 --> 00:04:07.063
The young man entered the room.

69
00:04:08.244 --> 00:04:13.168
Gillenormand didn't know what to say, so he said rather abruptly, "What do you want?"

70
00:04:13.188 --> 00:04:16.070
"Monsieur Monsieur?

71
00:04:16.750 --> 00:04:18.972
Gillenormand couldn't believe what he was hearing.

72
00:04:19.912 --> 00:04:25.417
Marius should rush to him and embrace him, not stand across the room, calling him "monsieur."

73
00:04:25.437 --> 00:04:28.179
"Did you come to beg my pardon?"

74
00:04:28.759 --> 00:04:30.041
interrupted Gillenormand.

75
00:04:30.641 --> 00:04:32.222
"Have you realized you were wrong?"

76
00:04:32.242 --> 00:04:33.984
"No, monsieur."

77
00:04:34.004 --> 00:04:35.925
"Then what do you want from me?"

78
00:04:36.326 --> 00:04:39.929
exclaimed Gillenormand in a voice filled with grief and rage.

79
00:04:39.949 --> 00:04:44.272
"Father, I know that my presence here is upsetting you.

80
00:04:44.853 --> 00:04:48.516
But I came to ask only one thing of you and then I will go away."

81
00:04:49.616 --> 00:04:53.879
That one word, "father," was all Gillenormand needed to hear.

82
00:04:53.899 --> 00:04:56.380
"What can I do for you, my son?"

83
00:04:57.541 --> 00:05:01.863
Marius explained that he wanted his grandfather's permission to marry Cosette.

84
00:05:03.504 --> 00:05:05.285
Gillenormand looked him up and down.

85
00:05:05.905 --> 00:05:09.407
It was obvious from the young man's clothes that he didn't have any money.

86
00:05:09.427 --> 00:05:12.369
"How much money do you make as a lawyer?"

87
00:05:12.389 --> 00:05:15.511
"I don't work as a lawyer," Marius replied.

88
00:05:15.531 --> 00:05:17.592
"So the girl is rich?

89
00:05:18.148 --> 00:05:20.810
She must have a large dowry," said Gillenormand.

90
00:05:21.991 --> 00:05:23.773
"Oh no, she doesn't have any dowry.

91
00:05:24.353 --> 00:05:25.654
She's an orphan like me."

92
00:05:26.675 --> 00:05:27.796
Gillenormand laughed.

93
00:05:27.816 --> 00:05:32.700
"Then, my dear boy, love this girl but don't marry her.

94
00:05:33.481 --> 00:05:34.561
Keep her as your mistress."

95
00:05:35.582 --> 00:05:36.983
"Never!" said Marius.

96
00:05:37.003 --> 00:05:39.866
"You have insulted Cosette by even suggesting that.

97
00:05:40.506 --> 00:05:41.807
Good evening, monsieur."

98
00:05:42.728 --> 00:05:47.372
And with that, he turned and fled his grandfather's house for the second time in his life.

99
00:05:48.680 --> 00:05:52.782
That same afternoon Jean Valjean was sitting behind a slope.

100
00:05:54.463 --> 00:06:00.525
He wore a workingman's waistcoat, brown linen trousers, and a cap with a large visor that hid his

101
00:06:00.565 --> 00:06:06.348
face. Valjean was anxious about a number of things and needed time alone to think.

102
00:06:07.528 --> 00:06:10.810
A while ago he had spotted Thenardier near his house.

103
00:06:11.410 --> 00:06:15.932
Thanks to Valjean's disguise as a workingman, the villain had not recognized him.

104
00:06:16.660 --> 00:06:23.202
Valjean had seen Thenardier again several times, and he was now certain that Thenardier was prowling about the

105
00:06:23.242 --> 00:06:27.963
neighborhood. This alone was enough to make Valjean consider leaving Paris.

106
00:06:28.663 --> 00:06:31.184
But there were other things preying on his mind.

107
00:06:32.144 --> 00:06:37.666
There was political unrest in Paris, which meant that the police were more alert than usual.

108
00:06:38.806 --> 00:06:42.467
Valjean feared that they might find him when they were arresting other people.

109
00:06:43.467 --> 00:06:48.458
So he had decided to leave France with Cosette, but he was worried about getting a passport.

110
00:06:49.359 --> 00:06:53.322
In addition, something strange had happened that very morning.

111
00:06:54.703 --> 00:07:00.408
Valjean had been walking in the garden at dawn when he came across a message: 16 Rue de

112
00:07:00.448 --> 00:07:01.289
la Verrerie.

113
00:07:02.089 --> 00:07:03.831
The words were scratched upon a wall.

114
00:07:04.852 --> 00:07:08.014
hey looked quite fresh, so they must have been written overnight.

115
00:07:08.615 --> 00:07:09.576
But what was this?

116
00:07:10.216 --> 00:07:10.817
An address?

117
00:07:11.437 --> 00:07:12.418
A warning for him?

118
00:07:13.159 --> 00:07:14.220
A signal for others?

119
00:07:15.232 --> 00:07:19.434
Valjean had no idea, but it was obvious that someone had been in the garden.

120
00:07:20.374 --> 00:07:22.975
He remembered Cosette's sighting of the shadow with the hat.

121
00:07:23.576 --> 00:07:27.277
It would probably be wise not to mention this latest mystery to her.

122
00:07:28.358 --> 00:07:33.280
In the midst of these thoughts, he sensed that someone was standing on the slope behind him.

123
00:07:33.900 --> 00:07:37.702
He was about to turn around when a piece of paper fell at his feet.

124
00:07:38.802 --> 00:07:41.984
Valjean jumped up just in time to see someone running away.

125
00:07:42.880 --> 00:07:48.603
This person was larger than a child but smaller than a man, and dressed in a gray smock

126
00:07:48.663 --> 00:07:50.024
and brown velvet pants.

127
00:07:50.945 --> 00:07:56.528
Valjean picked up the paper and read a message scrawled in large letters: Move out.
